# SpecialLogicColumns.config
# Pipe-delimited rules used by CodeGenNew.SchemaIntrospection to pre-compute special-logic
# flags onto TableModel/ColumnModel. See Docs/specs.md section 5.2 and section 7 for the
# full explanation. Lines starting with # are comments.
#
# Format:
#   category|flagcolumn(s)|companioncolumn(s)|special
#
# Matching rules per name pattern:
#   Pattern*   -> StartsWith
#   *Pattern   -> EndsWith
#   *Pattern*  -> Contains
#   Pattern    -> exact match (no wildcard)
# Multiple patterns in one slot are comma-separated; matching any one counts as a match.
#
# Rule kind:
#   companioncolumn(s) blank     -> per-column classification (flag pattern alone -> per-column bool)
#   companioncolumn(s) populated -> table-level paired-column rule (needs a column matching each side)
#
# special:
#   Comma-separated flags. Currently only IgnoreCase is defined (case-insensitive matching).
#   Blank means case-sensitive matching.
#
# The spCanDelete dependency check on delete actions is NOT a column-name-pattern rule and is
# intentionally not represented here (see specs.md section 7).
#
# CreateDateColumn / ModifiedDateColumn / LastChangedDateColumn / InactiveReasonColumn are per-column
# classification rules (companion blank): any matching column is excluded from generated Update
# parameters entirely.
#   - CreateDateColumn match: never touched by Update logic at all (only set on Insert).
#   - CreateUserColumn match (e.g. CreateUser): unlike the date columns it IS a caller-supplied Insert (and
#     Save) parameter -- the caller says who is creating the row -- but it is excluded from Update
#     parameters entirely, so the original creator is never overwritten. (Deliberately only "CreateUser*":
#     "CreatedBy"-style names already match CreateDateColumn's "Created*" pattern.)
#   - ModifiedDateColumn match: set to GETDATE() unconditionally by generated Update logic.
#   - LastChangedDateColumn match: a blend -- set alongside CreateDate on Insert, and set alongside
#     ModifiedDate on Update. For Update logic specifically it behaves just like ModifiedDateColumn
#     (GETDATE() unconditionally); the Insert template will also set it alongside CreateDate.
#   - InactiveReasonColumn match (e.g. InactiveReasonNoteText): excluded from Insert parameters and
#     left NULL -- doesn't make sense to give a newly-inserted row an inactive reason.
#   - DisplayColumn (a per-column rule, but the pattern ORDER is a priority): the columns a Lookup shows so a person can
#     recognize a row -- what the referencing table shows about each foreign-keyed row, and what the row itself shows.
#     Primary-key and foreign-key columns are never display columns (they are the IDs, returned separately).
#     Every matching column of a table is shown (in table order); the earliest-listed pattern that matches also decides
#     the Lookup's ORDER BY. If a table has NO matching column, its first ordinary string column is used instead.
#     Tune the list to your naming (put the column you most want to sort/recognize by first). Never applied to
#     text/ntext/xml/binary columns.
#
# IsActiveFlag's flag-column patterns cover BOTH polarities -- "IsActive"/"Active" (1 = active) and
# "IsInactive" (1 = inactive, the opposite sense). SP_Insert.tt reads the matched column's own name to
# tell which polarity it is and hardcodes the correct "active by default" literal (1 for IsActive-style,
# 0 for IsInactive-style) rather than taking it as a parameter -- a newly inserted row should never be
# born inactive. The companion InactiveDate/InactivatedAt column is excluded from Insert entirely (left NULL).
#
# IsActiveFlag / StartEndDate / SoftDelete were case-SENSITIVE matching until a survey of ~35 real
# databases on this box (2026-09-22) turned up widespread ALL-CAPS and snake_case naming in older schemas
# (BEGIN_DATE, END_DATE, IsInActive, DELETED_ON, ...) that the exact-case patterns below silently missed.
# All three are now IgnoreCase, matching every other category. A few exact snake_case spellings that came
# up repeatedly (Is_Active, Is_Deleted, ...) were also added directly, since the */Pattern* wildcard engine
# has no "ignore underscores" mode.
#
# ModifiedUserColumn (e.g. ModifiedBy, UpdatedBy) was added for the same reason -- "who last touched this
# row" is a very common companion to ModifiedDateColumn in the wild. It mirrors CreateUserColumn: a normal
# caller-supplied parameter on Update (the caller says who is editing), excluded from Insert entirely
# (nothing to modify yet on a brand-new row) and from SP_Load/SP_Clone's copy (seed rows and fresh clones
# have no prior editor). Its patterns ("ModifiedBy*"/"UpdatedBy*") were chosen so they cannot also match
# ModifiedDateColumn's own patterns -- the two categories should never claim the same column.

IsActiveFlag|IsActive,Active,IsInactive,ActiveFlag,Is_Active,Active_Flag,Is_Inactive|InactiveDate,InactivatedAt,Inactive_Date,Date_Inactivated|IgnoreCase
StartEndDate|Start*,Begin*,Hire*,Origin*,Open*,*Opened|Stop*,End*,Termination*,Final*,Close*,*Closed|IgnoreCase
SoftDelete|IsDeleted,Deleted,Is_Deleted|DelDate,DeletedDate,DeletedAt,Deleted_On,Date_Deleted|IgnoreCase
CreateDateColumn|CreateDate*,Created*,DateCreated,CreatedOn,Creation*,CreateTime,Date_Created,Created_On||IgnoreCase
CreateUserColumn|CreateUser*||IgnoreCase
ModifiedDateColumn|ModifiedDate*,ModDate*,UpdatedDate*,LastModified*,DateModified,ModifiedOn,Date_Modified,Modified_On,Date_Updated,Updated_On,UpdatedOn,Date_Last_Modified,Last_Modified_Date||IgnoreCase
ModifiedUserColumn|ModifiedBy*,UpdatedBy*||IgnoreCase
LastChangedDateColumn|LastDateChanged,LastChanged*,DateLastChanged||IgnoreCase
InactiveReasonColumn|InactiveReason*,InactiveNote*||IgnoreCase
AdminFlagColumn|IsAdmin||IgnoreCase
DisplayColumn|ShortDescr,ShortDescription,Name,*Name,Descr,Description,*Description,Title,Label,*Number,*Code||IgnoreCase
